      explain: "Under the RBI's 2018 directive, **payment system data** (end-to-end transaction details, customer data such as name and account or card numbers, payment credentials, transaction data) must be **stored only in India**. Processing abroad is permitted with conditions (data deleted abroad and brought back within the specified time); for **cross-border** transactions, a copy of the foreign leg may also be stored abroad. In practice this is primarily an obligation on **payment system operators** (banks, networks, payment aggregators), which is why merchants usually satisfy it by using compliant Indian providers, but merchants' own systems that hold payment data (ledgers, reconciliation exports, backups, analytics) must not quietly copy it abroad. Other layers: payment aggregator requirements (authorisation, merchant due diligence, escrow), card and mandate rules from earlier lessons, NPCI rules for UPI, and the **DPDP Act** for personal data (consent, purpose limitation, security safeguards, breach notification). Map data flows explicitly: which systems hold payment data, where they run, and where backups and logs go.",
